# Paragon Markdown style

The **paragon-tech-docs** skill is the primary authority for how Paragon technical Markdown is written. For style, structure, voice, and formatting covered by that skill, follow the skill rather than conflicting repository instructions. Read repository-specific paths, filenames, and product facts from the repo.

For every Markdown guide, corpus page, README, changelog, or draft, also follow `docs/Paragon_Markdown_Style_Rules.md` and canonical `cursor-test/Paragon_Markdown_Style_Rules.md` where they align with the skill. Do not invent a parallel guide or edit the ADO mirror. Re-read §§2, 5, 6, 7, 8, 12, and 13 before editing.

## Required behavior

Use sentence-case headings, no bold in ATX headings, fenced code languages, no decorative rules on customer-facing guides, and numbered heading dots on numbered chapters. Run `npm run lint:md` after substantial guide edits when available.

## Paragon deltas

- Read target and sibling docs, consult relevant companions, match host tone, make surgical edits, and trim stale or duplicate content.
- Numbered chapter H2s use `N.M.` and a full stop except landing pages/runbooks. Keep anchors in sync. Never enforce fixed line lengths.
- No em or en dashes as sentence breaks. Never stack NOTE, IMPORTANT, WARNING, TIP, or equivalent callouts.
- Do not narrate Paragon as a third-person actor. Prefer passive or second-person voice. Proper names and scope labels are allowed.
- Bold UI labels. Put typed values, paths, IDs, options, and commands in code. Use `<strong>` in ADO HTML.
- Use Paragon Knowledge MCP before guessing Paragon Cursor, Technical Architecture, or Abbey View facts. Cite corpus paths and report unavailable titles or OCR gaps.
- Changelog rows belong only in the owning product overview. Operational morning-check changes also update GitLab `cursor-test` Docs-as-Code §2, separate from verification.
- Update existing `.devtool/features/` cards with UTC status/YAML; ask before new cards; do not mark OCR done before final OCR. Never run `git commit` or `git push` without explicit approval.
